---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on router1: its BGP daemon (bgpd) is not running, even though a
correct BGP configuration exists in /etc/frr/frr.conf. The config file defines
router1 in AS 1, peering with router2 at 193.10.11.2 (AS 2), and advertising
the 195.11.14.0/24 prefix. However, because bgpd is not running, no BGP session
is ever established. As a result, router1's RIB contains only its two directly
connected routes and has no path to 200.1.1.0/24 (pc2's subnet). When pc1 sends
traffic to 200.1.1.2, router1 immediately returns an ICMP Destination Net
Unreachable — exactly what the user sees. On the peer side, router2's BGP daemon
is fully operational; it is advertising 200.1.1.0/24 correctly but its neighbor
entry for 193.10.11.1 is permanently stuck in the Active state, which is the
expected symptom when the remote BGP speaker never opens a TCP session on port
179. router2 is completely healthy and does not need to be restarted. The root
cause is that the bgpd process on router1 is not running despite a valid
configuration being present on disk. The corrective fix is to restart the FRR
suite on router1 (e.g., `systemctl restart frr`), which will start bgpd, load
the frr.conf, establish the eBGP session with router2, exchange prefixes, and
restore end-to-end connectivity between pc1 and pc2.
=== END ===
